1
00:00:00,450 --> 00:00:03,417
Aarav's toy car stops every
time it bumps into a wall.

2
00:00:03,717 --> 00:00:04,745
He opens it up.

3
00:00:04,745 --> 00:00:06,885
A wire has come loose from the board.

4
00:00:07,185 --> 00:00:08,664
Tape will not hold it.

5
00:00:08,664 --> 00:00:10,413
Glue does not carry current.

6
00:00:10,413 --> 00:00:12,413
So how do we join it for good?

7
00:00:12,913 --> 00:00:13,979
We solder it.

8
00:00:13,979 --> 00:00:18,073
Soldering joins metal parts using
a melted metal, called solder.

9
00:00:18,423 --> 00:00:20,600
Think of it as a glue made of metal.

10
00:00:20,600 --> 00:00:23,103
It holds the parts,
and it carries current.

11
00:00:23,753 --> 00:00:27,325
Solder is a soft mix of
metals, called an alloy.

12
00:00:27,675 --> 00:00:31,768
The classic solder is sixty
percent tin and forty percent lead.

13
00:00:32,118 --> 00:00:36,148
It melts at about one hundred and
eighty three to one hundred and ninety

14
00:00:36,148 --> 00:00:37,198
degrees Celsius.

15
00:00:37,848 --> 00:00:42,237
Lead free solder, made of tin with a
little silver and copper, melts higher.

16
00:00:42,237 --> 00:00:44,359
About two hundred and twenty degrees.

17
00:00:45,009 --> 00:00:48,709
Metal surfaces carry a thin
dirty layer, called an oxide.

18
00:00:49,059 --> 00:00:50,893
Solder will not stick to it.

19
00:00:50,893 --> 00:00:52,024
So we use flux.

20
00:00:52,374 --> 00:00:56,556
Flux cleans the oxide away,
so the solder flows and grips.

21
00:00:56,556 --> 00:00:58,973
Most solder wire has flux inside.

22
00:00:59,623 --> 00:01:01,111
The tools are simple.

23
00:01:01,461 --> 00:01:03,720
A soldering iron has a hot metal tip.

24
00:01:03,720 --> 00:01:06,416
Twenty five watts is
common for small circuits.

25
00:01:06,766 --> 00:01:08,661
It always rests in a stand.

26
00:01:08,661 --> 00:01:10,952
A damp sponge wipes the tip clean.

27
00:01:11,302 --> 00:01:14,824
For taking joints apart, we use a
desoldering pump, and desoldering wick.

28
00:01:15,474 --> 00:01:17,937
Now, let's make one
joint on the breadboard.

29
00:01:18,287 --> 00:01:21,333
First, wipe the tip, and
add a little solder to it.

30
00:01:21,333 --> 00:01:22,698
This is called tinning.

31
00:01:23,048 --> 00:01:27,278
Next, touch the tip to the copper
pad and the wire, both together.

32
00:01:27,628 --> 00:01:29,590
Watch the joint temperature climb.

33
00:01:29,940 --> 00:01:34,637
When it passes the melting point, feed
the solder into the joint, not onto

34
00:01:34,637 --> 00:01:35,537
the iron.

35
00:01:35,791 --> 00:01:39,545
The solder flows all round,
and forms a smooth cone.

36
00:01:39,895 --> 00:01:41,559
Take the solder away first.

37
00:01:41,559 --> 00:01:42,549
Then the iron.

38
00:01:42,549 --> 00:01:44,450
Do not move it while it cools.

39
00:01:45,100 --> 00:01:46,407
Now test the joint.

40
00:01:46,407 --> 00:01:49,030
A lamp and a scope check if current flows.

41
00:01:49,380 --> 00:01:50,825
A bad joint flickers.

42
00:01:50,825 --> 00:01:52,797
The voltage keeps dropping out.

43
00:01:53,147 --> 00:01:54,856
A good joint is steady.

44
00:01:54,856 --> 00:01:57,078
The voltage stays at five volts.

45
00:01:57,728 --> 00:01:59,706
So what does a good joint look like?

46
00:02:00,056 --> 00:02:03,504
A good joint is shiny, and
shaped like a small cone.

47
00:02:03,854 --> 00:02:06,314
A dull, lumpy ball is a cold joint.

48
00:02:06,314 --> 00:02:08,468
The solder never gripped the pad.

49
00:02:08,818 --> 00:02:11,461
A blob joining two pads
is a solder bridge.

50
00:02:11,461 --> 00:02:13,079
It makes a short circuit.

51
00:02:13,729 --> 00:02:15,618
To remove a part, we desolder.

52
00:02:15,968 --> 00:02:17,027
Melt the joint.

53
00:02:17,027 --> 00:02:19,933
Press the plunger of the
pump, then release it.

54
00:02:20,283 --> 00:02:23,161
It sucks the hot solder up, like a straw.

55
00:02:23,811 --> 00:02:25,986
Or press copper wick on the joint.

56
00:02:25,986 --> 00:02:29,529
The braid soaks up the solder,
like a sponge soaks water.

57
00:02:30,179 --> 00:02:31,841
And always solder safely.

58
00:02:32,191 --> 00:02:34,727
Put the iron back in its stand every time.

59
00:02:35,077 --> 00:02:39,385
Work in a room with fresh air, and
keep the fumes away from your face.

60
00:02:39,735 --> 00:02:42,773
Wear safety glasses,
because hot solder can spit.

61
00:02:43,123 --> 00:02:46,910
And wash your hands after work,
especially with lead solder.

62
00:02:47,560 --> 00:02:49,475
Heat the joint, not the solder.

63
00:02:50,275 --> 00:02:51,339
Worked example.

64
00:02:51,339 --> 00:02:55,929
A soldering iron is rated twenty five
watts, two hundred and thirty volts.

65
00:02:56,279 --> 00:02:58,132
Current, I equals P by V.

66
00:02:58,132 --> 00:02:59,880
Twenty five by two thirty.

67
00:02:59,880 --> 00:03:02,016
About zero point one one amperes.

68
00:03:02,716 --> 00:03:05,918
Element resistance, R
equals V squared by P.

69
00:03:06,618 --> 00:03:10,185
Two thirty squared is fifty
two thousand nine hundred.

70
00:03:10,185 --> 00:03:11,811
Divide by twenty five.

71
00:03:12,511 --> 00:03:16,027
That is about two thousand
one hundred and sixteen ohms.

72
00:03:16,727 --> 00:03:20,088
In one minute, energy is
twenty five times sixty.

73
00:03:20,088 --> 00:03:21,678
Fifteen hundred joules.

74
00:03:22,328 --> 00:03:23,789
Now, the exam trap.

75
00:03:24,139 --> 00:03:27,834
Many students melt solder on the
tip, and drip it onto the joint.

76
00:03:28,184 --> 00:03:29,084
Wrong.

77
00:03:28,750 --> 00:03:31,998
The joint stays cold, and
the solder does not grip.

78
00:03:31,998 --> 00:03:33,622
That makes a cold joint.

79
00:03:34,272 --> 00:03:35,172
Right.

80
00:03:34,820 --> 00:03:38,338
Heat the pad and the lead,
then feed solder to the joint.

81
00:03:38,988 --> 00:03:40,610
Where is this asked?

82
00:03:40,610 --> 00:03:42,470
In PUC board papers.

83
00:03:42,820 --> 00:03:46,795
Expect the steps of soldering, the
role of flux, and desoldering tools.

84
00:03:47,445 --> 00:03:48,345
Try this.

85
00:03:48,234 --> 00:03:52,697
A two hundred and thirty volt soldering
iron has an element of one thousand

86
00:03:52,697 --> 00:03:54,178
and fifty eight ohms.

87
00:03:54,178 --> 00:03:55,515
What is its power?

88
00:04:00,915 --> 00:04:01,815
B.

89
00:04:01,333 --> 00:04:02,323
Fifty watts.

90
00:04:02,673 --> 00:04:04,647
P equals V squared by R.

91
00:04:04,997 --> 00:04:08,244
Two thirty squared is fifty
two thousand nine hundred.

92
00:04:08,594 --> 00:04:11,183
Divide by one thousand and fifty eight.

93
00:04:11,183 --> 00:04:12,656
That is fifty watts.

94
00:04:13,306 --> 00:04:14,852
Screenshot this card.

95
00:04:18,052 --> 00:04:20,049
Next, printed circuit boards.

96
00:04:20,049 --> 00:04:23,231
